-- title:
--   $\delta$-PAC strategy for fixed-confidence best arm identification
-- statement:
--   A **strategy** for best arm identification with $K$ arms consists of a sampling rule $\pi$ (the arm $A_t$ drawn at round $t$ may depend, possibly through extra randomization, on the observations of the first $t-1$ rounds), a stopping time $\tau$ of the natural filtration $\mathcal F_t = \sigma(A_1, X_1, \dots, A_t, X_t)$, possibly infinite, and a decision rule $\psi$ recommending an arm.
--
--   Given $\delta \in \mathbb R$ and a class $\mathcal E$ of bandit models, the strategy is **$\delta$-PAC on $\mathcal E$** if for every model $\nu \in \mathcal E$:
--
--   1. it stops almost surely: $\mathbb P_\nu(\tau < \infty) = 1$;
--   2. it errs with probability at most $\delta$:
--   $$\mathbb P_\nu\big(\tau < \infty \text{ and } \psi \text{ recommends an arm whose mean is below the largest mean of } \nu\big) \le \delta .$$
--
--   Here $\mathbb P_\nu$ is the law of the infinite trajectory $(A_1, X_1), (A_2, X_2), \dots$ generated by $\pi$ in the model $\nu$. On a model with a unique optimal arm $a^*$, clause 2 together with clause 1 is exactly $\mathbb P_\nu(\psi \ne a^*) \le \delta$, the paper's definition.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The protocol is the platform's (`BanditAlgorithm.banditTrajMeasure`, `IsBanditStoppingTime`, `IsSoundBAI`); clause 2 is `IsSoundBAI`, stated as an outer-measure bound. Being a stopping time and the $\mathcal F_\tau$-measurability of $\psi$ are separate hypotheses of the theorems that use this definition.
-- source:
--   Garivier, Kaufmann, Optimal Best Arm Identification with Fixed Confidence, arXiv:1602.04589v2, p. 3, §2.1 (definition of a $\delta$-PAC strategy) and p. 1, §1 (sampling rule, stopping rule, decision rule)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BanditTrajectory

open MeasureTheory

namespace OptimalBAI.LowerBound

/-- A strategy `(π, τ, ψ)` — sampling rule `π`, stopping time `τ`, decision rule `ψ` — is
`δ`-PAC on the class `𝓔` of bandit models (Garivier–Kaufmann, arXiv:1602.04589v2, p. 3, §2.1) if
for every model `ν ∈ 𝓔`:
* the strategy stops almost surely, `ℙ_ν(τ < ∞) = 1`; and
* it errs with probability at most `δ`: `ℙ_ν(τ < ∞ and ψ recommends a suboptimal arm) ≤ δ`
  (`BanditAlgorithm.IsSoundBAI`).
On a model with a unique optimal arm `a*`, "`ψ` is suboptimal" is "`ψ ≠ a*`", and together with
the first clause the second is the paper's `ℙ_ν(â_τ ≠ a*) ≤ δ`. -/
def IsDeltaPAC {K : ℕ} (δ : ℝ) (π : BanditAlgorithm.BanditPolicy K)
    (τ : (ℕ → Fin K × ℝ) → ℕ∞) (ψ : (ℕ → Fin K × ℝ) → Fin K)
    (𝓔 : Set (BanditAlgorithm.StochasticBandit K)) : Prop :=
  (∀ ν ∈ 𝓔, ∀ᵐ ω ∂BanditAlgorithm.banditTrajMeasure ν π, τ ω < ⊤) ∧
    BanditAlgorithm.IsSoundBAI δ π τ ψ 𝓔

end OptimalBAI.LowerBound


